Z||000013||WKDFIRO||05-06-2001||11:33 PM||||The different Dukes does bring up a good point to consider. The 88 Dukes had the counter-rotating balance shafts but Ive never been able to feel the difference between an 87 Duke (wo shafts) and an 88. To my thinking, your Duke's power would be taxed more if it has shafts to rotate. I refer to the power drop in the Quad 4s when they got the shafts (mechanically speaking!)The DIS system in my experience is almost foolproof and as reliable as the sun rising in the East!<P>That in mind...<BR>There have been plenty of 84-86 Dukes with aftermarket ignitions, aftermarket intakes and TBI (Holley), correct me if Im wrong anyone, but I would think that the roller cam and lifters should be able to plug right in with no problems. <P>So what would I pick? If power were the only thing in the equation I would go with the 86 Duke. <P>BTW the Super Dukes were putting out 330 hp in the Fiero GTP that won the 87 Daytona 24 hour enduro. But it also probably had the DOHC heads that were available from Pontiac Motor Sports. The few heads that were made were created to also fit the normal Duke block. <P>3 liters, with 4 valves, hows THAT for the best of both worlds?<P>Hot Rod magazine had a great write up on it...<P>  ||||reg||1
